{"language":"FCL-1","records":[
    {"id":"r13","type":"claim","text":"Both the lack of documented chore agreements and deeper relational dynamics (power imbalances, accumulated resentment, differing conflict styles) drive the household disputes.","depends":["p.response.0#r1","p.response.1#r8","p.response.1#r11"]},
    {"id":"r14","type":"objection","text":"A shared chore calendar can become a source of policing unless explicit ground‑rules and a culture of non‑blame are established.","depends":["p.response.0#r5","p.response.1#r9"]},
    {"id":"r15","type":"problem","text":"How can the household embed relational safety and emotional openness into a chore‑coordination tool so that procedural clarity does not create new conflict?"},
    {"id":"r16","type":"commitment","text":"Set up a shared digital chore board (e.g., Google Sheet or Trello) that lists all recurring tasks, allows anyone to claim or swap slots, and includes a visible log of changes. Attach ground‑rules: no blame language, updates are informational, and any concern triggers a brief "feelings" check‑in.","scope":"household","action":"install board and publish rules","depends":["p.response.0#r5","r14"]},
    {"id":"r17","type":"commitment","text":"Schedule a 10‑minute weekly chore check‑in (e.g., after dinner) with a rotating facilitator. The agenda includes task status, any overloads, and a 2‑minute space for each person to share how they feel about the workload.","scope":"household","action":"plan weekly check‑in","depends":["p.response.2#r2","p.response.1#r11"]},
    {"id":"r18","type":"commitment","text":"Introduce a non‑verbal cue system on the board (colored stickers, emoji tags) that lets a roommate indicate \"busy\" or \"need help\" without a direct conversation.","scope":"household","action":"add cue icons to board","depends":["p.response.2#r3"]},
    {"id":"r19","type":"use","text":"Use the digital board and cue system to track chore completion and signal needs in real time.","scope":"ongoing","action":"track chores and cues","depends":["r16","r18"]},
    {"id":"r20","type":"commitment","text":"If after two weeks relational tension remains high, agree to a mediated discussion (e.g., with a neutral third party) or consider revisiting the living arrangement.","scope":"household","action":"plan escalation step","depends":["r15"]}
